# MyNet.Avalonia Performance Rules

# Core Principle

Performance is a first-class concern in MyNet.Avalonia.

However, performance must never compromise:

- API clarity
- maintainability
- correctness
- developer experience

Optimize only when it is justified and measurable.

---

# Primary Performance Philosophy

Prefer:

- simplicity
- predictability
- composability

Avoid premature optimization.

Always design for clarity first, then optimize hot paths when needed.

---

# UI Performance Model (Avalonia Context)

Performance in Avalonia is mainly impacted by:

- visual tree complexity
- binding evaluation frequency
- layout recalculations
- template inflation
- event subscriptions
- allocations in rendering paths

Always consider UI lifecycle impact when designing controls.

---

# Visual Tree Rules

## Avoid unnecessary depth

Deep visual trees should be avoided when possible.

Prefer:

- flat structures
- composition
- lightweight controls

Deep nesting increases:

- layout cost
- rendering cost
- memory usage

---

## Avoid frequent tree traversal

Do NOT repeatedly traverse the visual tree.

Avoid:

- `GetVisualDescendants`
- repeated `VisualTreeHelper` usage
- runtime lookups in layout/render loops

Cache results when necessary.

---

# Binding Performance Rules

## Prefer compiled bindings

Always prefer:

- compiled bindings
- strongly typed bindings

Avoid:

- reflection-based bindings in hot paths

---

## Avoid excessive bindings

Each binding has a cost.

Avoid:

- large numbers of bindings per control
- unnecessary property bindings
- nested binding chains

Prefer computed properties when appropriate.

---

## Avoid binding-heavy templates

In high-frequency controls (lists, grids):

- minimize bindings per item
- reuse templates efficiently
- avoid deep binding graphs

---

# Collection Controls (Critical Section)

For ItemsControl / List-like controls:

- prefer virtualization when applicable
- avoid per-item heavy logic
- avoid allocations in item templates
- keep item templates lightweight

Never introduce:

- per-item services
- per-item subscriptions without cleanup
- heavy converters in item bindings

---

# Allocation Rules

## Avoid allocations in hot paths

Avoid:

- LINQ in UI loops
- closures in event handlers
- repeated object creation in layout/render

Prefer:

- cached delegates
- static methods
- reusable objects
- pooling where appropriate

---

## Avoid hidden allocations

Be aware of:

- string concatenation in bindings
- implicit boxing
- lambda captures

---

# Event Subscription Rules

## Always unsubscribe

All event subscriptions must be cleaned up.

Failing to unsubscribe causes:

- memory leaks
- unexpected behavior
- performance degradation

---

## Avoid excessive subscriptions

Avoid subscribing to:

- frequently fired events unnecessarily
- global events without filtering

Prefer scoped subscriptions.

---

# Layout Performance Rules

## Avoid unnecessary invalidation

Do not trigger layout invalidation unless necessary.

Avoid:

- frequent property changes causing layout recalculation
- cascading layout updates

---

## Minimize layout complexity

Layout should be:

- predictable
- shallow
- composable

Avoid complex custom panels unless necessary.

---

# Template Performance Rules

## Avoid heavy template initialization

Template creation should be:

- fast
- lightweight
- deterministic

Avoid:

- runtime tree scanning in OnApplyTemplate
- expensive initialization logic in templates

---

## Cache template parts

Template parts should be cached once:

- in `OnApplyTemplate`
- not re-resolved repeatedly

---

# Rendering Rules

Avoid:

- unnecessary redraw triggers
- frequent property invalidation
- excessive visual state changes

Prefer batched updates when possible.

---

# Control Lifecycle Performance

Be mindful of:

- `OnAttachedToVisualTree`
- `OnDetachedFromVisualTree`
- `OnApplyTemplate`

Do not perform heavy work repeatedly in lifecycle methods.

---

# Memory Management Rules

## Prevent memory leaks

Always clean up:

- event handlers
- timers
- subscriptions
- reactive streams

---

## Avoid long-lived object retention

Do not accidentally capture:

- controls
- view models
- services in closures

---

# Styling Performance

Avoid:

- overly complex style selectors
- deep style inheritance chains
- expensive runtime style resolution

Prefer:

- simple styles
- reusable styles
- explicit styling rules

---

# Attached Properties Performance

Attached properties must:

- be lightweight
- avoid side effects in setters
- avoid triggering heavy logic on change

Never use them for heavy runtime computation.

---

# Converter Performance

Converters should:

- be stateless
- be lightweight
- avoid allocations
- avoid external dependencies

Avoid complex logic in converters.

---

# Source of Inefficiency (Common Pitfalls)

Watch for:

- excessive bindings per control
- per-item allocations in lists
- repeated visual tree queries
- unnecessary control creation/destruction
- heavy templates
- uncontrolled event subscriptions

---

# Optimization Strategy

## Step 1: Design First

Always design for clarity first.

---

## Step 2: Measure (if needed)

Optimize only when:

- performance issue is observable
- bottleneck is identified

---

## Step 3: Targeted Optimization

Optimize:

- hot paths
- item controls
- layout loops
- binding-heavy components

---

# Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

- premature micro-optimizations
- over-engineered caching layers
- unnecessary pooling
- complex state machines for UI performance

---

# Final Principle

A performant MyNet.Avalonia control is:

- simple
- predictable
- composable
- lightweight in bindings
- efficient in templates
- free of unnecessary allocations in hot paths